  <main id="main" class="wrap">
    <section class="page-intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">Documentation</p>
      <h1>The language,<br>on one page.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Zhex's features — and the editor tooling around them — with setup guides for syntax highlighting.</p>
      <p class="aside">This page summarizes what works today. The full contract lives in the repository at <a href="https://github.com/desireco/zhex/blob/main/docs/initial-language.md">docs/initial-language.md</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="features-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">The language</p><h2 id="features-title">Small words, checked types.</h2></div>
        <p class="intro">Every program is checked — names, types, and word connections — before any of it runs. Blocks are four-space indentation; no braces, no semicolons.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="docs-feature">
        <h3>Words with typed signatures.</h3>
        <p>A <strong>word</strong> is a function. Parameter and result types are explicit, and the final statement supplies the result.</p>
        <div class="command-block"><pre><code>{{`word greet(name: String) -> String
    "Hello, " + name + "!"` | highlight}}</code></pre></div>
      </div>

      <div class="docs-feature">
        <h3>Named values.</h3>
        <p><code>let</code> creates an immutable value; <code>var</code> allows local updates. Types are inferred, and arithmetic on 64-bit integers is checked — overflow is an error, not a surprise.</p>
        <div class="command-block"><pre><code>{{`let greeting = "Hi"
var attempts = 0
attempts = attempts + 1` | highlight}}</code></pre></div>
      </div>

      <div class="docs-feature">
        <h3>Flow, made visible.</h3>
        <p>A <code>flow</code> block passes each result to the next word, one per line. The seed can be any expression — <code>flow gcd(a, b)</code> starts from a computed value. The checker verifies every connection before execution; direct calls like <code>greet(name)</code> work too.</p>
        <div class="command-block"><pre><code>{{`flow name
    greet
    print` | highlight}}</code></pre></div>
      </div>

      <div class="docs-feature">
        <h3>Decisions.</h3>
        <p><code>if</code>, optional <code>elif</code>, and optional <code>else</code> select a branch. Conditions must be <code>Bool</code>, and branch results must match.</p>
        <div class="command-block"><pre><code>{{`if n % 15 == 0
    "FizzBuzz"
elif n % 3 == 0
    "Fizz"
else
    int.to-string(n)` | highlight}}</code></pre></div>
      </div>

      <div class="docs-feature">
        <h3>Bounded iteration.</h3>
        <p><code>for</code> iterates upward over integers with an exclusive end. No lists are built; bounds evaluate once.</p>
        <div class="command-block"><pre><code>{{`for n in range(1, limit + 1)
    flow n
        fizzbuzz
        print` | highlight}}</code></pre></div>
      </div>

      <div class="docs-feature">
        <h3>Console input, honestly handled.</h3>
        <p><code>console.read-line</code> and <code>console.read-int</code> return a result that can fail. Postfix <code>try</code> unwraps success and propagates failure — input never silently becomes zero or an empty string.</p>
        <div class="command-block"><pre><code>{{`let n = console.read-int("Number: ") try
print(n * n)` | highlight}}</code></pre></div>
      </div>

      <div class="docs-feature">
        <h3>Standard operations.</h3>
        <p>The predeclared words: <code>print</code>, <code>range</code>, <code>int.to-string</code>, <code>text.reverse</code>, <code>console.read-line</code>, and <code>console.read-int</code>. <code>+</code> adds integers and concatenates strings; there are no implicit conversions.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="docs-feature">
        <h3>Study working programs.</h3>
        <p>The examples page renders each program with real input and output. <a href="examples.html#gcd">GCD</a> shows recursion and a flow seeded with a computed call; the <a href="examples.html#times-table">times table</a> shows a bounded loop building strings from integers.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="editors-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Editor support</p><h2 id="editors-title">Syntax highlighting, per editor.</h2></div>
        <p class="intro">Local packages bring Zhex highlighting to Vim and Neovim, VS Code, and Zed — with four-space indentation and <code>#</code> comments built in.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="docs-feature">
        <h3>Vim and Neovim.</h3>
        <p>Standard Vim syntax files — no Tree-sitter needed. The installer links this checkout into your pack directories; repeating it is safe.</p>
        <div class="command-block"><pre><code>git clone https://github.com/desireco/zhex.git
cd zhex
just vim-install</code></pre></div>
        <p class="access-note">Keep the checkout at its current path. Open a <code>.zhex</code> file and <code>:set filetype?</code> should report <code>zhex</code>.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="docs-feature">
        <h3>VS Code.</h3>
        <p>TextMate highlighting, parentheses and quote pairing, and indentation rules. Build the extension with a current Node.js LTS release and npm.</p>
        <div class="command-block"><pre><code>cd editors/vscode
npm ci
npm test
npm run package
code --install-extension ../../dist/editors/zhex-0.0.1.vsix</code></pre></div>
        <p class="access-note">You can also run <strong>Extensions: Install from VSIX</strong>. Open a <code>.zhex</code> file and confirm the language mode is <strong>Zhex</strong>.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="docs-feature">
        <h3>Zed.</h3>
        <p>Tree-sitter grammar with bracket matching and a word-definition outline. Prepare a dev-extension folder, then install it from disk.</p>
        <div class="command-block"><pre><code>just zed-prepare
# Zed → Extensions → Install Dev Extension → select dist/editors/zed</code></pre></div>
        <p class="access-note">Needs Go 1.23 or newer and Git; writes only generated files under <code>dist/</code>. Zed builds the grammar and recognizes <code>.zhex</code> files as <strong>Zhex</strong>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="example-notes">
      <p class="eyebrow">Worth knowing</p>
      <h2>Where tools stop.</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Editors highlight statements line by line; the interpreter remains responsible for validating indentation, scope, and types.</li>
        <li>Themes choose the highlighting colors; the packages provide the structure.</li>
        <li>The packages are local for now. Extension-store publication, language-server completion, live compiler diagnostics, and formatting are future work.</li>
      </ul>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/desireco/zhex/blob/main/docs/initial-language.md">Read the full language contract →</a>
    </section>
